This is the **constructive half** of Theorem 2.12 before the biconditional is stated: from injectivity alone (Corollary 1.6, Sierpiński power, idempotent fixed points, Propositions 2.9–2.10) one builds a **continuous lattice** and a **homeomorphism** to it with **Scott** topology. See [Idempotent fixed points](1972_IdempotentFixedPoint.md) for the fixed-point subtype used in the proof. The full iff is [Theorem 2.12](1972_Theorem_2_12.md); when a lattice order and topology are already given and specialization equals $\le$, see [Specialization equals the given complete-lattice order on injective T₀ spaces](1972_Specialization_eq_lattice_on_injective.md).

**Theorem (`injective_homeomorph_to_continuousLatticeScott`).** If $D$ is an injective $T_0$ space, then there exist a complete lattice $E$, a **`ContinuousLattice`** structure on $E$, and a homeomorphism $D \cong E$ where $E$ carries its **Scott** topology.

**Proof.** Retract $D$ into a Sierpiński power; form the idempotent $e = s \circ r$; take **`IdempotentFixedPoint e`** with **`completeLattice`**, **`isContinuousLattice`**, and **`scottTopology`**; homeomorph $D$ to the fixed-point set as a subspace. $\square$
